   "3...dxe4": "Black gives up the centre to free the position.",
   "4...Bf5": "The bishop develops with tempo, attacking the knight on e4.",
   "5.Ng3": "The knight moves and attacks the bishop in return.",
   "6.h4": "White threatens h5, trapping the bishop.",
   "6...h6": "Black makes room: the bishop can retreat to h7.",
   "7...Nd7": "Black prepares ...Ngf6 without allowing Ne5."
  },
  "explanation": "In the Classical Caro-Kann Black gets a healthy position with no bad pieces: the light-squared bishop comes out before ...e6. White uses the h-pawn to gain space and push the bishop back. Both sides then usually castle on opposite sides.",
