  "final_fen": "r1bq1b1r/ppp3pp/2n1k3/3np3/2B5/2N2Q2/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K2R b KQ - 3 8",
  "notes": {
   "5...Nxd5": "Natural, but it allows a knight sacrifice.",
   "6.Nxf7": "The knight takes on f7, forking queen and rook.",
   "6...Kxf7": "Black accepts. The king is pulled forward.",
   "7.Qf3+": "Check, and the queen attacks the knight on d5.",
   "7...Ke6": "The king must defend the pinned knight on d5 itself.",
   "8.Nc3": "More pressure on d5. Black's king is stuck in the centre."
  },
  "explanation": "The Fried Liver is a famous sacrifice: White gives a knight on f7 so that Black's king has to walk into the centre. The knight on d5 is pinned to the king by the bishop on c4, and White keeps adding pressure with Qf3+ and Nc3. It's a great example of attacking an uncastled king.",
